  <span class="genre-pill">Literary Fiction</span>
  <h1>The Question</h1>
  <div class="story-meta">April 2026</div>
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>Later, in bed, she falls asleep quickly, which she does, and he lies awake and looks at the ceiling and thinks.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>What he thinks about is the question. Not the specific question he asked himself in the library, but the category of question — the kind of wondering that has no answer, the kind of curiosity that does not resolve into knowledge. He has spent his adult life building a life that minimizes these questions. He has built it carefully, room by room, the way you build a house to keep the weather out. And the weather stays out. And the house is warm. And he is comfortable.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>And something, he thinks, is missing.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Not something large. Not something dramatic. A small thing, a quiet thing, the size of a question that goes unasked. The thing that the explorer was talking about, maybe — not the waiting itself, but what you find when you stop filling the waiting with noise. The self that is underneath all the busyness, the one that has things to say if anyone ever listens.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He does not know how to listen to that self. He has not practiced in decades.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He lies in the dark for a long time. Then he sleeps.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>On Saturday he goes for a walk. This is not his habit. He is not a walker. But the morning is clear and cool and the neighborhood is doing the Saturday things it always does — lawns being mowed in patches, children on bicycles, the quiet commerce of a life that is in no particular hurry. He walks past the school, past the library where he sat in the dust and the light, past the deli and the bank and the veterinary clinic.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He walks until he is in a part of the neighborhood he does not know well. The houses are older here. The trees are bigger. There is a small park he has never been to — a rectangle of grass with a few benches and an old fountain that is not running. He sits on one of the benches.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>A woman is sitting on the other bench, reading a book. She is perhaps seventy, with white hair pulled back and glasses on a chain around her neck. She looks up and nods at him, the way neighbors do, and then goes back to her book. He sits.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He watches a squirrel navigate a oak tree. He watches a child learn to ride a bicycle, her father jogging beside her with one hand on the seat, the bicycle wobbling and correcting, the child laughing each time she almost falls and doesn't. He watches the fountain that is not running — the basin full of brown leaves, the spout pointing at the sky.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He thinks: this is the kind of thing I used to notice. He thinks: when did I stop noticing?</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He does not know. The noticing went somewhere, the way things go — slowly, then all at once, until one day you look up and the world is still there, still full of squirrels and children and fountains and light, and you are inside your warm house and the door is closed and you have not seen any of it.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He sits on the bench for an hour. The woman with the book leaves. Other people come and go — a young couple with a dog, an old man with a cane who sits and feeds the pigeons from a paper bag. No one speaks to anyone. No one needs to. The park is doing what parks do: holding space for the quiet presence of people who are not in any particular hurry.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>When he leaves, he feels the way you feel after a long sleep — not rested, exactly, but still. The kind of still that comes from having been somewhere without needing to do anything while you were there.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Sunday. His wife is grading papers at the kitchen table. He is doing the dishes, which is part of the Saturday and Sunday routine — she cooks on Saturdays, he does dishes; he cooks on Sundays, she dries. A system. A good system. The house smells like coffee.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He dries his hands and opens a drawer he has opened a thousand times — the junk drawer, the one with the batteries and the tape and the scissors and the birthday candles that have been there so long they have turned yellow with age. He moves some things aside. Underneath, there is a small notebook. It is hers, he thinks — a small reporter's notebook with a soft cover. He picks it up.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He opens it. The first several pages have her handwriting on them — shopping lists, notes, the kind of thing she jots down without thinking. He flips to a blank page near the back.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He stands in the kitchen for a moment, holding the pen, looking at the blank page.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He thinks about what he might write. A list of things he has wondered about and stopped wondering about. A description of the fountain that is not running. A story about a man who goes to a park and watches a squirrel and does not write any of it down. He does not know what the story would be about, exactly. He is not sure stories need to be about anything in particular. He is not even sure he can write one.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>But the page is there. And the pen is there. And the question — not what do I want but do I still have the capacity to want anything at all — is there, sitting in his chest like the first day of spring, like the first time you step outside and the air is warm and you realize, without knowing how you realized it, that winter is over.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He starts to write.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Not because he knows what he is writing. Not because he has something important to say. He starts to write because the page is there and the pen is in his hand and for the first time in longer than he can remember, he has a question he is not trying to answer.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He writes: <em>I used to ask questions all the time. I stopped. I'm not sure why. I'm not sure when.</em></p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He stops. He looks at what he wrote. It is not much. It is not a story. It is barely a sentence. But it is his, and it is true, and it is the kind of thing that only gets written if you sit down and write it, the way the squirrel only gets watched if you are sitting still enough to watch.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He keeps writing.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The page fills. The next page fills. His wife looks up once and says, "What are you working on?" and he says, "Nothing," which is true and not true, and she goes back to her papers, and he goes back to his page, and the kitchen is quiet and smells like coffee and the morning is doing what mornings do — it passes, slowly, without drama, carrying with it the ordinary weight of a life that is still, he is beginning to think, capable of wonder.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The notebook goes in the junk drawer when he is done. Not because it belongs there but because it needs to be somewhere it will not be lost, somewhere it will wait.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He leaves the drawer open.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
